        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Day 1 : Si Sa Ket",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Huai Thap Than Khmer Ruins (Ban Prasart Khmer Ruins) / Khao Phra Viharn National Park / Wat Maha Phuttharam",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Sirinthon Dam / Pakmun Dam / Kaeng Tana National Park",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Kaeng Sam Phan Bok / Pha Taem National Park / Hat Salueng / Wat Si Ubon Rattanaram (Wat Si Thong) / Maenam Song Si Viewpoint",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Huai Thap Than Khmer Ruins (Ban Prasart Khmer Ruins)",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Khao Phra Viharn National Park",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "The park’s territory lines Thailand-Cambodia border, over high cliff and lush forest of Dongrak Range. Khao Phra Wihan National Park acquires 130 square kilometres area of Amphoe Kantharalak of Si Sa Ket, Amphoe Nam Khun and Amphoe Nam Yuen of Ubon Ratchathani. The country’s 83rd National Park was established on March 20, 1998.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "It is a dam made of stone piling and clay. It was built to obstruct Mun river at Baan Hua Wae, Khong Chiam district. Its height is 17 m. Its length is 300 m. It facilitates farming and electricity generating.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Kaeng Tana National Park",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Kaeng Sam Phan Bok",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Pha Taem National Park",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "This park covers an area of 140 square kilometers, where plateaus and hills dominate the parks landscape. There are sheer cliffs, which resulted from earthquakes. Most trees are of the deciduous dipterocarp forest. Irregular shaped sandstone is found scattered throughout the area.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Wat Si Ubon Rattanaram (Wat Si Thong)",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Maenam Song Si Viewpoint",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Maenam Song Si means the two-colored river or the Mun Rivers alluvium. It is the spot where the brown water of the Mekong River and the blue water of the Mun River join.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
